use warnings;
use 5.042;
use lib::findbin '../lib'; # dev-only
use App::crcsum;

exit App::crcsum->run(@ARGV);

# PODNAME: crcsum
# ABSTRACT: Perl port of dos-crcsum16

=head1 SYNOPSIS

 crcsum [FILE]...
 crcsum -c [MANIFEST]...

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Print or check CRC-32 (ZIP/Ethernet) checksums, like C<md5sum>.  This
is a Unix port of C<dos-crcsum16>, and uses the same manifest format,
so a manifest made on one can be checked on the other.

Each file gives one line: eight lower case hex digits, a space, C<*>,
and the file name.  With no FILE, or when FILE is C<->, standard input
is read.

  $ crcsum *.exe > crc32.sum
  $ crcsum -c crc32.sum
  BCOM.EXE: OK
  TICO.EXE: OK

The CRC is CRC-32/ISO-HDLC: reflected polynomial C<0xEDB88320>, initial
value and final XOR C<0xFFFFFFFF>.  The values match Python's
C<zlib.crc32> and the CRCs stored in ZIP files.  The plain C<cksum>
command uses a different CRC.  CRC-32 detects accidental corruption; it
is B<not> cryptographically secure.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item -c, --check

Read checksums from the MANIFESTs and verify them.

=item -h, -?, --help

Show help.

=item --version

Show the version.

=back

=head1 MANIFESTS

C<crcsum -c> reads lines of the form

 <8 hex digits><space><* or space><file name>

Files are opened relative to the current directory.  Manifests may use
LF, CR LF or CR line endings, and the last line need not be terminated.
Hex digits may be either case.  Blank lines and lines starting with
C<#> are skipped, and a Ctrl-Z ends the manifest.  A file listed more
than once is checked each time.

Each entry prints C<OK>, C<FAILED> (wrong checksum) or C<FAILED open or
read>.  Malformed lines and a summary of failures go to standard error;
the remaining entries are still checked.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

=over 4

=item C<0>

Every file was read and, when checking, every checksum matched.

=item C<1>

A file was missing, unreadable or did not match.

=item C<2>

Usage error, unreadable manifest, manifest with malformed lines or no
entries, or a write error on standard output.

=back

=cut
